---
import Doc from '../layouts/Doc.astro';
import LineChart from '../components/LineChart.astro';
import RunDetails from '../components/RunDetails.astro';
import Term from '../components/Term.astro';
import m1 from '../../public/data/runs/m1_01_ambient_levels.json';
import v01 from '../../public/data/runs/p1_01_forward_validation.json';
import am from '../../public/data/runs/p1_04_ambient.json';
import lib from '../../public/data/ambient.json';
import pz from '../../public/data/runs/p2_00_ambient_giza.json';
import PlanMap from '../components/PlanMap.astro';
import bud from '../../public/data/runs/p2_05_budget.json';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

const z = m1.components.BHZ;
const per = z.period_s as number[];
const keep = per.map((p, i) => [p, i] as const).filter(([p]) => p >= 0.1 && p <= 30);
const px = keep.map(([p]) => p);
const curve = (k: string) => keep.map(([, i]) => (z.acc_db as Record<string, number[]>)[k][i]);
const pet = m1.peterson;
const bands = Object.entries(z.band_rms_um_s as Record<string, Record<string, number>>);
const micro = z.band_rms_um_s['0.1-0.3 Hz'];
const cult = z.band_rms_um_s['1.0-3.0 Hz'];
const nhnmMicro = pet.band_rms_um_s['0.1-0.3 Hz'].high;
const floor = 77; // µm/s: the first investigation's velocity floor (legacy R4); Phase 2 showed it is the scatter of a blind reading
// the smallest motion one dwell could detect, every advantage allowed (P2-05), µm/s
type BRow = { case: string; target: string; a_min_m_s: number | null };
const bRows = bud.rows as BRow[];
const reach = (c: number, t: number) => ((bRows.find((r) => r.case === bRows[0 + 4 * c].case && r.target === bRows[t].target)?.a_min_m_s ?? 0) as number) * 1e6;
const plainReach = reach(0, 1);
const brightReach = reach(0, 2);
const truckGroundReach = reach(3, 1);
const cR = v01.rayleigh.exact_m_s as number;
const fFor = (size: number) => cR / (2 * size); // half a wavelength across the target
const lamFreq = [0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 1, 2, 5, 10, 20, 50, 100, 200];
const hund = (v: number) => (Math.round(v / 100) * 100).toLocaleString('en-US');
const cul = lib.cultural as unknown as Record<string, { vdb?: number; value?: number; quote?: string; status?: string }>;
const culRows = [
  ['urban_background', 'Typical background vibration'],
  ['bus_or_truck_typical', 'Bus or truck, typical'],
  ['human_perception', 'Threshold of human perception'],
  ['bus_or_truck_over_bump', 'Bus or truck over a bump'],
].map(([k, label]) => ({ label, vdb: cul[k].vdb!, um: cul[k].value! * 1e6, status: cul[k].status! }));
const sig = (v: number) => (v >= 10 ? v.toFixed(0) : v >= 1 ? v.toFixed(1) : v.toPrecision(2));
---
<Doc
  title="The ground's own motion"
  kicker="Ambient · measured"
  current="ambient"
  lede="Without a hammer the ground still moves: ocean swell felt as microseisms, wind, traffic, footsteps. It is what ambient-noise seismology listens to and what the radar claim says it reads. How much it moves near Giza, and at what frequencies, sets what either can see. Here it is measured, not assumed."
  state="status · measured near Giza · used by the satellite simulation as the ground’s motion"
  index={[
    { id: 'levels', label: 'Levels' },
    { id: 'spectrum', label: 'Spectrum' },
    { id: 'giza', label: 'Giza’s frequencies' },
    { id: 'resolution', label: 'What each band sees' },
    { id: 'cultural', label: 'Near roads' },
    { id: 'plateau', label: 'The plateau' },
    { id: 'radar', label: 'Against the radar' },
    { id: 'next', label: 'Next' },
  ]}
>
  <section id="levels">
    <h2>Near Giza the ground moves by hundredths of a micrometre per second on its own</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      The nearest open broadband record is MedNet station KEG at Kottamya, 67 km east of Khafre: an STS-1 seismometer in a
      7 m vault on a limestone hill. Twelve days of 1996, one in the middle of each month, three components, instrument
      response removed, turned into power spectra the way network operators do. Its <Term k="microseism">microseisms</Term> are the region’s, Giza’s
      included; above 1 Hz a quiet hill is a lower bound for a plateau at the edge of a city of twenty million.
    </p>
    <div class="stat-row">
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{micro.all_p50.toFixed(3)}<small>µm/s</small></span><span class="what">median vertical ground velocity in the microseism band, 0.1–0.3 Hz</span><span class="src">10th–90th percentile {micro.all_p10.toFixed(3)}–{micro.all_p90.toFixed(3)}</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{cult.all_p50.toFixed(3)}<small>µm/s</small></span><span class="what">median at 1–3 Hz, by day {cult.day_p50.toFixed(3)}, by night {cult.night_p50.toFixed(3)}</span><span class="src">local time, UTC+2</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{nhnmMicro.toFixed(1)}<small>µm/s</small></span><span class="what">the microseism band at the world’s noisiest stations, Peterson’s high-noise model</span><span class="src">Peterson 1993, Table 4</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{Math.round(fFor(6))}<small>Hz</small></span><span class="what">the lowest frequency whose half wavelength fits a 6 m chamber, where only nearby feet and machines make noise</span><span class="src">Rayleigh speed {Math.round(cR).toLocaleString('en-US')} m/s</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="spectrum">
    <h2>A quiet site: well above the global floor, far below the noisiest stations</h2>
    <div class="grid-2 charts">
      <LineChart
        title="Fig. A1 · Vertical ground acceleration spectrum at Kottamya, 1996"
        series={[
          { name: 'Peterson high', x: pet.period_s, y: pet.high_db, color: 'var(--muted)', dash: '5 3', width: 1.3 },
          { name: '90th percentile', x: px, y: curve('all_p90'), color: 'var(--verdigris)', width: 1, dash: '2 2' },
          { name: 'median', x: px, y: curve('all_p50'), color: 'var(--verdigris)', width: 2.2 },
          { name: '10th percentile', x: px, y: curve('all_p10'), color: 'var(--verdigris)', width: 1, dash: '2 2' },
          { name: 'Peterson low', x: pet.period_s, y: pet.low_db, color: 'var(--muted)', dash: '5 3', width: 1.3 },
        ]}
        xLabel="period, s"
        yLabel="dB re 1 (m/s²)²/Hz"
        logX
        xTicks={[0.1, 1, 10]}
        xDomain={[0.1, 30]}
        height={250}
        directLabels={false}
        caption={`${z.windows} one-hour windows · the microseism peak near 3–5 s; above 2 Hz a day–night difference of a few dB, the mark of people`}
      />
      <div>
        <table class="data">
          <thead><tr><th>Band</th><th class="r">Kottamya, median</th><th class="r">10th–90th</th><th class="r">Peterson low</th><th class="r">high</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              bands.map(([b, v]) => (
                <tr>
                  <td class="mono">{b}</td>
                  <td class="num">{sig(v.all_p50)}</td>
                  <td class="num">{sig(v.all_p10)}–{sig(v.all_p90)}</td>
                  <td class="num">{sig((pet.band_rms_um_s as Record<string, { low: number; high: number }>)[b].low)}</td>
                  <td class="num">{sig((pet.band_rms_um_s as Record<string, { low: number; high: number }>)[b].high)}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="note">RMS vertical ground velocity in each band, µm/s, integrated from the spectra. Peterson’s low-noise model is a composite of the quietest stations on Earth, “unlikely to be duplicated at any single location”; his high-noise model is the average of the noisiest.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="finding">{m1.finding}</p>
    <RunDetails manifest={m1.manifest} extra={[['station', 'MN.KEG Kottamya, 29.9275 N 31.8292 E, 460 m, STS-1 at 7 m depth, open data at INGV'], ['method', 'McNamara & Buland (2004) power spectral densities via ObsPy PPSD'], ['bounds', 'Peterson (1993), USGS Open-File Report 93-322, Tables 3 and 4']]} />
  </section>

  <section id="giza">
    <h2>Giza’s ground rings near 0.6 Hz, and the Great Pyramid near 2.3 Hz</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      An ambient-noise survey of Khufu’s pyramid, 37 points in its chambers, on its blocks and on the ground around it
      (ELGabry and others, <em>Scientific Reports</em> 16, 14032, 2026), found the pyramid’s fundamental frequency between
      2.0 and 2.6 Hz, “with an average of ~ 2.3”, and “the average predominant frequency on the surrounding ground is about
      0.60 Hz”. The survey reports spectral ratios, not absolute levels. We have found no open, absolute measurement of
      ambient motion on the plateau itself; the city’s traffic will raise it above Kottamya’s above about 1 Hz, by an
      amount still to be sourced.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="resolution">
    <h2>The ground’s own waves are kilometres long where they are strong, and metres long only where they are local</h2>
    <div class="grid-2 charts">
      <LineChart
        title="Fig. A2 · Rayleigh wavelength in Giza limestone against frequency"
        series={[
          { name: 'wavelength', x: lamFreq, y: lamFreq.map((f) => cR / f), color: 'var(--verdigris)', width: 2 },
          { name: 'claimed depth, 648 m', x: [0.1, 200], y: [648, 648], color: 'var(--porphyry)', dash: '4 3', width: 1.2 },
          { name: 'claimed shafts, 10 m', x: [0.1, 200], y: [10, 10], color: 'var(--porphyry)', dash: '2 2', width: 1.2 },
          { name: 'bench chamber, 6 m', x: [0.1, 200], y: [6, 6], color: 'var(--lapis)', dash: '2 2', width: 1.2 },
        ]}
        xLabel="frequency, Hz"
        yLabel="metres"
        logX
        logY
        yFormat="num"
        xTicks={[0.1, 1, 10, 100]}
        height={250}
        directLabels={false}
        caption={`λ = c / f with the Rayleigh speed of the bench rock, ${Math.round(cR).toLocaleString('en-US')} m/s (P1-01); an image resolves about half a wavelength`}
      />
      <div class="note-card">
        <h3>What each band can see</h3>
        <p>In the microseism band, where the ground moves most, a wave is 6 to 17 km long: it feels the average of kilometres of rock. At Giza’s 0.6 Hz resonance it is about 3 km. To resolve a 6 m chamber takes about {Math.round(fFor(6))} Hz; a 10 m shaft about {Math.round(fFor(10))} Hz.</p>
        <p>At those frequencies there is no regional noise field, only sources within tens of metres: footsteps, engines, pumps. That is the setting of the ambient-noise imaging test, which even then, with the rock known exactly and an unlimited record, reaches only {am.score.image_contrast_ensemble.toFixed(1)}× at the chamber (<a href={`${base}/seismic/#ambient`}>P1-04</a>).</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="cultural">
    <h2>Fifteen metres from a road, a passing truck shakes the ground a thousand times harder than the microseisms</h2>
    <div class="grid-2 charts">
      <div>
        <table class="data">
          <thead><tr><th>At 15 m (50 ft) from the source</th><th class="r">VdB</th><th class="r">µm/s</th><th class="r">× microseisms</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              culRows.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{r.label}</td>
                  <td class="num">{r.vdb}</td>
                  <td class="num">{sig(r.um)}</td>
                  <td class="num">{hund(r.um / micro.all_p50)}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="note">RMS velocity over one second, above 8 Hz, from the Federal Transit Administration’s <em>Transit Noise and Vibration Impact Assessment</em> (1995), Figure 7-3 (levels read from the figure; the perception threshold quoted from the text). VdB is 20 log₁₀ of the velocity over 10⁻⁶ in/s.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <h3>Local, brief, and above 8 Hz</h3>
        <p>Traffic is the strongest ambient source a site like Giza has, and it is local: it falls off within tens of metres of the road, comes and goes with each vehicle, and lives above 8 Hz, “for all practical purposes the frequency range of interest to ground-borne vibration” in the manual’s words.</p>
        <p>A truck over a bump reaches about {sig(culRows[3].um)} µm/s. The satellite simulation answered whether a dwell can follow motion that fast: a look short enough to follow 15 Hz lasts 33 ms and blurs the ground over 33 m, and at that speed the smallest motion one pass could detect on plain ground is about {Math.round(truckGroundReach / 1000).toLocaleString('en-US')},000 µm/s (<a href={`${base}/satellite/#budget`}>P2-05</a>).</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="plateau">
    <h2>Near the roads the ground shakes hardest, and still out of the satellite’s reach</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      Worst case by construction: every mapped road carries a truck at its point nearest each spot, with the manual’s
      levels 15 m away, falling off as a surface wave does (Caltrans 2020, Eq. 6) between undamped rock and the most
      damping rock-like ground in its tables. Roads from OpenStreetMap. The map marks where a truck would shake the ground
      faster than {floor} µm/s, the first investigation’s velocity floor, which the satellite simulation showed is the scatter of a reading
      blind to ground motion (<a href={`${base}/satellite/#record`}>R4, corrected</a>). A typical truck never does; a truck
      over a bump does within {Math.round(pz.cases['bump · rock'].reach_m)} m of the road.
    </p>
    <div class="grid-2 charts">
      <PlanMap
        title="Fig. A3 · The Giza plateau: roads, and where traffic would shake the ground faster than 77 µm/s"
        box={pz.plan.box}
        roads={pz.plan.roads}
        pyramids={pz.plan.pyramids}
        reach={pz.cases['bump · rock'].reach_m}
        caption="the site's extent, 1.6 × 1.4 km; the town of Nazlet el-Samman lies along the east edge"
      />
      <div>
        <table class="data">
          <thead><tr><th>A truck on the nearest road</th><th class="r">at 15 m, µm/s</th><th class="r">above 77 µm/s within</th><th class="r">of the plateau</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              Object.entries(pz.cases).map(([k, c]) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{k.replace('typical', 'typical truck').replace('bump', 'over a bump').replace('rock', 'undamped rock').replace('marly chalk', 'damping as marly chalk')}</td>
                  <td class="num">{c.level_15m_um_s.toFixed(0)}</td>
                  <td class="num">{c.reach_m > 0 ? `${c.reach_m.toFixed(0)} m` : 'never'}</td>
                  <td class="num">{(100 * c.area_fraction_above_floor).toFixed(0)}%</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="note">From each pyramid’s centre to the nearest mapped road: {Object.entries(pz.distance_to_nearest_road_m).map(([k, d]) => `${k[0].toUpperCase() + k.slice(1)} ${Math.round(d as number)} m`).join(' · ')}. Within 15 m of a road the level is held at its 15 m value: closer is louder, and unsourced.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="finding">{pz.finding}</p>
    <RunDetails manifest={pz.manifest} extra={[['roads', `${pz.manifest.params.roads} · as of ${pz.manifest.params.osm_timestamp}`], ['levels', 'FTA (1995) Figure 7-3 at 15 m; Caltrans (2020) Eq. 6, Table 1 exponent 0.5, Table 2 marly chalk 0.1 per metre'], ['worst case', pz.manifest.params.worst_case]]} />
  </section>

  <section id="radar">
    <h2>What one satellite pass could detect is {hund(brightReach / micro.all_p50)} to {hund(plainReach / micro.all_p50)} times Giza’s microseisms</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      The microseisms near Giza move the ground {micro.all_p50.toFixed(3)} µm/s; even Peterson’s high-noise model, the
      world’s noisiest stations, reaches {nhnmMicro.toFixed(1)} µm/s. Allowing every advantage, the smallest motion one ICEYE
      dwell could detect is about {Math.round(brightReach)} µm/s on a bright stone and {hund(plainReach)} µm/s on plain ground
      (<a href={`${base}/satellite/#budget`}>P2-05</a>), and a chamber changes the ground’s motion by only a
      ten-thousandth of it (<a href={`${base}/satellite/#imprint`}>P2-04</a>). The first investigation’s {floor} µm/s
      “floor” (R4) was the scatter of a reading that recovers 1% of real ground motion, and its 0.1–10 µm/s for ambient
      motion had no source; both are replaced here by measurement.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="next">
    <h2>If the seismic lab continues: the ambient field in every site</h2>
    <div class="grid-3">
      <div class="note-card">
        <h3>Microseisms</h3>
        <p>Surface waves from distant storms, arriving from every direction as plane waves at 0.1–1 Hz: generated to the measured spectrum, with the Rayleigh ellipse for horizontal motion and the regional speed (about 3 km/s; Adam et al. 2025).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <h3>Cultural noise</h3>
        <p>Traffic on the mapped roads is now a sourced worst case (above). Next: time series with the traffic spectrum (10–30 Hz, centred near 15 Hz) and vehicles that come and go, for the 24.5 s of a dwell; a measured level on the plateau would replace the manual’s.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <h3>One field, three uses</h3>
        <p>The ambient-noise test redone at realistic levels; the noise on the active surveys, replacing white noise; and the ground motion the simulated ICEYE dwell looks at.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Doc>

<style>
  .charts {
    margin-top: 12px;
    gap: 22px 28px;
    align-items: start;
  }
  .finding {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 13.5px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--text);
    max-width: 780px;
    margin-top: 16px;
    padding-left: 12px;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--verdigris);
  }
  .note {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 12.5px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-top: 10px;
  }
  .data .r {
    text-align: right;
  }
  .data td.mono {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .note-card p + p {
    margin-top: 8px;
  }
</style>
